# Programmatic ACF And Database Write Safety

Use this Rule when writing or reviewing any script or command that programmatically changes ACF field *definitions* (`acf-field` / `acf-field-group` posts, `acf_update_field()`, field `menu_order`) or writes directly to WordPress database tables (`$wpdb`, `update_post_meta`, raw SQL). It complements `wordpress-content-editing`, which covers editing page or post *values*.

A single re-order script has already deleted shared ACF source field definitions site-wide on a WESEO install. The loss was structural (definitions, not content), but it blocked editing every affected page. These rules make that class of error structurally hard.

<!-- acf-safety-reviewed: generic programmatic-write safety; routes WST/clone detail to wst-builder; no read-then-write-back idiom -->

## Treat Field-Definition Reads As Read-Only

- `acf_get_fields()` and `acf_get_field()` return an expanded, computed view, not an editable set of posts. On installs that use ACF clone fields (ACF Extended seamless clones), expanded entries can point at *shared* source field posts.
- Never iterate a field-list read and write the entries back with `acf_update_field()`. The "read the fields, change `menu_order`, write each entry back" loop is forbidden; on clone groups it destroys the shared source.
- Never `acf_update_field()` a field whose `key` is a composite (`field_<a>_field_<b>`) or that carries `_clone`, `__key`, or `__name`.

## Safe Write Shapes

- Add only your own new field with a fresh unique `key`; set `parent` to the numeric group post ID, never the `group_...` key string (a string parent silently becomes `parent=0`).
- Reorder by updating only the `menu_order` column of the group's real direct child `acf-field` posts via `$wpdb`. Do not bulk-reorder through the ACF API.
- For content values, prefer the project's ACF API (`update_field()` with the real field key) over raw `update_post_meta` or SQL, so compiled and performance caches stay consistent.

## Mandatory Preflight For Every Structural Write

- Snapshot the affected rows to a restore-capable file OUTSIDE the public webroot before writing (the target group plus any shared source groups for ACF definitions; the affected postmeta for content migrations).
- Separate dry-run from apply: first print exactly what would change, then apply only as a second, explicit run.
- Be idempotent: check existence (for ACF, `acf_get_field($key)`) before creating; never run a structural script blind twice.
- Keep a clear rollback path tied to the snapshot, and verify after the write (for ACF clone installs, run an integrity scan and confirm the editor and front end still render).
- Prefer admin import or a complete declarative definition (for ACF, `acf_import_field_group()`) over piecemeal mutation.

## WST And Clone Specifics

For WESEO WST and ACF Extended clone groups (Sections and CPT field groups that clone shared `[TMPL] ...` groups), the detailed forbidden patterns and the safe reorder, additive-field, snapshot, and clone-integrity-scan code live in the `wst-builder` Rule `acf-wst-patterns` and its reference. Apply those when the target install uses WST or ACF Extended clones.

This Rule never stores project-specific group keys, field post IDs, table prefixes, paths, or access details. Those belong in the project-local context or a concrete handoff.
